00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:45.350
But it's always
great to be here.

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:48.490
By the way, we also have
Twitter handles, #windowsongcp.

00:00:48.490 --> 00:00:51.310
So we're going to be tweeting
about Windows and .NET

00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:53.560
on Google Cloud
from this hashtag.

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:55.810
And I'm going to also share
my presentation with you

00:00:55.810 --> 00:00:58.870
on #windowsongcp at
#googlenext hashtags.

00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:01.390
So you don't have to take
pictures if you don't want to.

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:03.550
I'll share my presentation.

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:07.090
All right, so first of all,
it's a great time to be a .NET

00:01:07.090 --> 00:01:07.870
developer.

00:01:07.870 --> 00:01:10.000
For years and years and years--

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:13.010
by the way, who
is .NET over here?

00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:14.032
Great, yes.

00:01:14.032 --> 00:01:15.490
So you know what
I'm talking about.

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:18.850
For years and years, we've been
constrained to Windows world.

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:23.320
We have all this good stuff with
C#, Visual Studio, PowerShell,

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:24.850
SQL Server.

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:28.125
And it was frustrating for
me to just do that on Windows

00:01:28.125 --> 00:01:30.250
because I had to do stuff
on Linux once in a while,

00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:33.460
and I had to switch my
tools, switch my databases,

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:34.660
all that kind of stuff.

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:38.290
But now with ASP.NET Core,
you can target Linux.

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:40.660
With SQL Server
now, runs on Linux.

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:41.890
PowerShell runs on Linux.

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:45.280
Bash from Linux runs on Windows.

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:48.280
So it is truly a great time
to be a .NET developer.

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:51.430
And at Google, we are trying
to help with this revolution

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:52.180
with Google Cloud.

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:53.770
So this talk is
about that basically.

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:56.410
I just want to show you what
kind of stuff we are doing

00:01:56.410 --> 00:01:59.110
to help with .NET developers
running on Google Cloud.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:06.200
So the agenda today
is first, I want to--

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:09.759
well I'd like to start my
presentation with answering why

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:12.010
first before I show you how.

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:15.130
So the why here is, why should
you even care about Google

00:02:15.130 --> 00:02:17.521
Cloud when it comes
to .NET development?

00:02:17.521 --> 00:02:20.020
So I want to go through that
and make sure that it's crystal

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:22.897
clear to you guys why you should
even care about Google Cloud

00:02:22.897 --> 00:02:24.230
when it comes to .NET developer.

00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:26.830
And once we answer
that question,

00:02:26.830 --> 00:02:31.250
I want to talk about moving your
existing .NET apps to Google

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:31.750
Cloud.

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:33.640
So I'm going to show
you a simple app running

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:34.900
on my laptop.

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:37.849
And then we're going to look
at how we can take that app

00:02:37.849 --> 00:02:38.890
and move to Google Cloud.

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:42.380
So the point here is to show
you how easy it is, basically,

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:46.330
to have what you have
today and move it.

00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:50.012
Once we do that, once you have
your app running in the cloud,

00:02:50.012 --> 00:02:51.970
you have all these services
and all these tools

00:02:51.970 --> 00:02:53.950
that become available to you.

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:57.092
So we're going to take our
application to the next level.

00:02:57.092 --> 00:02:58.300
We want to use Cloud Storage.

00:02:58.300 --> 00:02:59.560
We're going to use
PowerShell cmdlets.

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:01.120
We're going to use
Visual Studio plug-ins.

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:02.710
We're going to use
machine learning

00:03:02.710 --> 00:03:05.260
with Vision API and
Speech API, and just

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:07.690
show you how easy it is, once
you have your app running

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:10.730
in the cloud, to
basically enhance

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:12.240
your application
with the services

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:14.470
that Google Cloud provides.

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:19.250
And then finally, it's fun
to work on fun features,

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:22.034
but then you need to make your
application production ready.

00:03:22.034 --> 00:03:23.200
So you need to have logging.

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:24.780
You need to have
error reporting.

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:25.990
You need to have autoscaling.

00:03:25.990 --> 00:03:27.810
You need to have load balancing.

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:31.585
These are boring tasks,
but everyone has to do it.

00:03:31.585 --> 00:03:33.460
So I just want to show
you what kind of tools

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:35.270
we provide to make
that easy for you.

00:03:35.270 --> 00:03:36.480
So this is the agenda.

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:42.440
So let's just answer
the question first.

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:44.315
The first question that
you probably have is,

00:03:44.315 --> 00:03:49.080
Windows Server, SQL
Server, ASP.NET on Google,

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:50.810
what's happening, right?

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:52.869
Because Google is
known for many things.

00:03:52.869 --> 00:03:54.410
Google is known for
machine learning.

00:03:54.410 --> 00:03:57.530
Google is known for search.

00:03:57.530 --> 00:03:59.665
Google is known for
Java and Python.

00:03:59.665 --> 00:04:03.700
We supported Java and Python
for a long time on App Engine.

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:07.140
We invented Go, and Go
is really popular now.

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:09.170
So when you think
about .NET and C#,

00:04:09.170 --> 00:04:11.270
you don't really think
about Google Cloud,

00:04:11.270 --> 00:04:12.880
up until recently.

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:14.780
And I think you are
right to think that way,

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:20.923
because if you look at the world
that we lived in up until 2014,

00:04:20.923 --> 00:04:22.089
it kind of looked like this.

00:04:22.089 --> 00:04:25.700
So on one side we had the Linux
world, the open source world,

00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:27.080
and the tools around it.

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:31.250
So for languages we
had Java, Python.

00:04:31.250 --> 00:04:33.290
We had Apache projects.

00:04:33.290 --> 00:04:35.390
We had Eclipse, IntelliJ as IDE.

00:04:35.390 --> 00:04:37.695
We had MySQL as your SQL server.

00:04:37.695 --> 00:04:39.050
You had Bash for scripting.

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:41.780
And there were a bunch
of other tools here.

00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:43.730
I'm just simplifying
a little bit.

00:04:43.730 --> 00:04:46.990
And then parallel to that,
we had the Windows world.

00:04:46.990 --> 00:04:49.310
Now with the Windows world,
you had similar things.

00:04:49.310 --> 00:04:50.930
So instead of Java, you had C#.

00:04:50.930 --> 00:04:53.570
Instead of Bash,
you had PowerShell.

00:04:53.570 --> 00:04:57.720
Instead of MySQL, you had SQL
Server, and so on and so forth.

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:01.190
And the unfortunate thing was
that these two worlds, they

00:05:01.190 --> 00:05:04.227
existed side by side, but
they never really interacted.

00:05:04.227 --> 00:05:05.810
And that was really
frustrating for me

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:08.690
because I started my career
as a Windows developer.

00:05:08.690 --> 00:05:10.160
I learned all these tools.

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:12.290
And then at some point
I had to switch my job.

00:05:12.290 --> 00:05:13.650
So I moved to Linux world.

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:16.520
I had to relearn everything.

00:05:16.520 --> 00:05:19.070
And you could argue
which side is better.

00:05:19.070 --> 00:05:20.162
I have my own opinions.

00:05:20.162 --> 00:05:22.370
Like for example I think C#
is much better than Java,

00:05:22.370 --> 00:05:24.060
for sure.

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:25.190
But that's not the point.

00:05:25.190 --> 00:05:27.050
The point is that
you were trying

00:05:27.050 --> 00:05:30.885
to solve similar problems
with almost similar tools,

00:05:30.885 --> 00:05:32.510
but they were slightly
different enough

00:05:32.510 --> 00:05:34.580
to make it annoying for you.

00:05:34.580 --> 00:05:36.346
But things are changing.

00:05:36.346 --> 00:05:38.720
If you look at what happened
in the last couple of years,

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:42.670
it's almost like a revolution.

00:05:42.670 --> 00:05:46.010
First, .NET went
open source in 2014.

00:05:46.010 --> 00:05:47.360
That was big news.

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:50.120
And then we gradually
started seeing

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:53.570
tools moving from Windows
to Linux and from Linux

00:05:53.570 --> 00:05:54.350
to Windows.

00:05:54.350 --> 00:05:58.560
Like for example, OpenSSH
moved from Linux to Windows.

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:00.000
So you could run
that on Windows.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:04.130
Then we had Ubuntu and
Debian running on Azure.

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:07.580
After that in 2016, it was
probably the biggest year

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:09.470
for .NET and Windows.

00:06:09.470 --> 00:06:13.100
We had the flagship SQL
Server now runs on Linux.

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:15.842
I mean if you think about that,
like three or four years ago,

00:06:15.842 --> 00:06:17.300
you couldn't even
think about that.

00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:18.830
And now it runs on Linux.

00:06:18.830 --> 00:06:21.570
Similarly with PowerShell,
the PowerShell cmdlets,

00:06:21.570 --> 00:06:22.940
they run on Linux.

00:06:22.940 --> 00:06:25.430
And then stuff also
moving from Linux--

00:06:25.430 --> 00:06:28.370
so Bash now runs on Windows.

00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:33.070
And lastly-- not
lastly, but ASP.NET Core

00:06:33.070 --> 00:06:34.680
is the most exciting
part for me.

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:37.460
So now you can write
your ASP.NET apps

00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:40.000
and target Linux,
Windows, and Mac.

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:42.110
And that's really exciting.

00:06:42.110 --> 00:06:46.040
And actually there's a talk
today later at 2:40 on .NET

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:47.720
containers that will cover this.

00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:50.570
At the end of my session, I'm
also going to point you to all

00:06:50.570 --> 00:06:52.861
the other talks and all the
other sessions that we have

00:06:52.861 --> 00:06:55.400
on .NET and Windows so
you know what we have.

00:06:55.400 --> 00:06:58.230
And then finally the year
ended with two announcements.

00:06:58.230 --> 00:07:00.840
The first one was Microsoft
joins Linux Foundation,

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:02.960
and then Google joins
.NET foundation.

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:04.490
I think, for me,
that was kind of

00:07:04.490 --> 00:07:06.350
like the highlight of the year.

00:07:06.350 --> 00:07:09.350
So now when you look at the
world that we're living today,

00:07:09.350 --> 00:07:12.150
we still have these two worlds.

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:14.120
So we still have two
operating systems.

00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:16.250
But then the tools and
languages around them,

00:07:16.250 --> 00:07:18.270
they are much more converged.

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:21.260
And this is really good news
for us because all the time

00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:25.217
and effort we spent in learning
C# and learning ASP.NET,

00:07:25.217 --> 00:07:27.050
learning PowerShell,
all that kind of stuff,

00:07:27.050 --> 00:07:29.044
now we can apply them to Linux.

00:07:29.044 --> 00:07:29.960
And that's great news.

00:07:32.690 --> 00:07:35.660
So now let's talk about
what Google Cloud is

00:07:35.660 --> 00:07:37.320
doing to help for this.

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:40.680
But before I do that, let's
answer the why question first.

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:42.980
So why should you even
deploy to Google Cloud?

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:45.800
And let's look at this question
from a consumer perspective.

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:47.390
So let's say you're
a cloud consumer.

00:07:47.390 --> 00:07:48.800
You are shopping around.

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:51.270
And Google Cloud tells you
that you should deploy here.

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:52.850
Why should you do that?

00:07:52.850 --> 00:07:55.520
There are many reasons,
but I think in my mind,

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:57.810
it boils down to
three main reasons.

00:07:57.810 --> 00:08:00.420
The first is Google's
infrastructure.

00:08:00.420 --> 00:08:05.924
So when you're running your
application on Google's cloud,

00:08:05.924 --> 00:08:08.090
you are basically running
on the same infrastructure

00:08:08.090 --> 00:08:09.912
that Google is
running, which means

00:08:09.912 --> 00:08:11.620
that you're running
on a private network,

00:08:11.620 --> 00:08:12.910
even between data centers.

00:08:12.910 --> 00:08:15.284
So if you go from one data
center to another data center,

00:08:15.284 --> 00:08:18.360
you are going through
Google's own payment network.

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:21.410
Which means that you have
good latency, good throughput,

00:08:21.410 --> 00:08:23.660
and it's very stable.

00:08:23.660 --> 00:08:25.880
So we have fast VM provisioning.

00:08:25.880 --> 00:08:29.510
So if you want a VM, it
becomes available to you

00:08:29.510 --> 00:08:30.934
within seconds.

00:08:30.934 --> 00:08:32.600
And that's really
important because when

00:08:32.600 --> 00:08:34.429
you are moving
applications to the cloud,

00:08:34.429 --> 00:08:35.809
you want things to be elastic.

00:08:35.809 --> 00:08:38.770
So you want to get VMs
as fast as possible.

00:08:38.770 --> 00:08:41.179
And also autoscale-- autoscale
is also really important.

00:08:41.179 --> 00:08:43.240
When you have lots
of load, you want

00:08:43.240 --> 00:08:45.920
autoscale to kick in
to add more machines.

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:47.510
And when the load
is gone, you want

00:08:47.510 --> 00:08:49.304
to scale down to save money.

00:08:49.304 --> 00:08:51.720
So the autoscaling at Google
Cloud, because of the network

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:53.510
and because of fast
VM provisioning,

00:08:53.510 --> 00:08:55.310
it really works really
fast and it really

00:08:55.310 --> 00:08:58.580
helps for scaling
up and scaling down.

00:08:58.580 --> 00:09:00.260
So that's number one.

00:09:00.260 --> 00:09:02.450
Number two is price.

00:09:02.450 --> 00:09:04.280
So if you're shopping
for something,

00:09:04.280 --> 00:09:06.020
you definitely care about price.

00:09:06.020 --> 00:09:08.000
So what we try to
do is first of all,

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:11.720
we try to keep our prices
as competitive as possible.

00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:15.860
But on top of that,
we have features that

00:09:15.860 --> 00:09:17.360
help you to save even more.

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:20.602
So for example, if you want
to run your VM for 10 minutes,

00:09:20.602 --> 00:09:21.560
you pay for 10 minutes.

00:09:21.560 --> 00:09:24.080
You don't pay for
the whole hour.

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:26.570
If you use your VM
for the whole month,

00:09:26.570 --> 00:09:28.154
you get automatic
discount, up to 30%,

00:09:28.154 --> 00:09:30.028
and you don't have to
do anything about that.

00:09:30.028 --> 00:09:32.390
We just detect that you run
the VM for the whole month,

00:09:32.390 --> 00:09:34.940
and we give you the
discount right away.

00:09:34.940 --> 00:09:39.380
If you have a VM that's
with lots of CPU and memory,

00:09:39.380 --> 00:09:42.350
but you're not really using
that, we detect your load

00:09:42.350 --> 00:09:43.880
and let you know.

00:09:43.880 --> 00:09:46.610
We tell you, by the way,
you can scale this VM down.

00:09:46.610 --> 00:09:48.190
You can decrease the memory.

00:09:48.190 --> 00:09:50.597
You can decrease the
CPU and run it cheaper

00:09:50.597 --> 00:09:51.680
because you don't need it.

00:09:51.680 --> 00:09:53.120
So we try to detect
what you need

00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:55.880
and tell you how you can save.

00:09:55.880 --> 00:09:58.340
And then the last
part is flexibility.

00:09:58.340 --> 00:10:01.820
So you can have
custom VMs, which

00:10:01.820 --> 00:10:04.310
means that you can
say, I want to have

00:10:04.310 --> 00:10:06.830
a VM with lots on memory
but not so much CPU,

00:10:06.830 --> 00:10:08.060
because that's what you need.

00:10:08.060 --> 00:10:10.550
Or you can say I want
a VM with lots of CPU

00:10:10.550 --> 00:10:11.747
but not so much memory.

00:10:11.747 --> 00:10:14.330
So you customize what you need,
and you pay for what you need.

00:10:14.330 --> 00:10:16.130
So that's great.

00:10:16.130 --> 00:10:21.320
And also in situations where
you have like a queue of work

00:10:21.320 --> 00:10:24.560
and you want to have VMs
just working from the queue,

00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:28.380
we have something
called preemptible VMs.

00:10:28.380 --> 00:10:30.482
These are VMs that
can come and go

00:10:30.482 --> 00:10:32.690
depending on the load in
other parts of Google Cloud.

00:10:32.690 --> 00:10:34.356
But the good thing
about them is they're

00:10:34.356 --> 00:10:37.310
perfect for this work queue
situation where you can just

00:10:37.310 --> 00:10:39.000
have 20 of them or 25 of them.

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:40.490
It doesn't really matter.

00:10:40.490 --> 00:10:44.180
But they're up to 80%
cheaper than the regular VMs.

00:10:44.180 --> 00:10:47.720
So these all combined, I
think, is a compelling reason

00:10:47.720 --> 00:10:50.030
why you should care about
Google Cloud as a consumer.

00:10:50.030 --> 00:10:51.960
But as a developer--

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:54.110
almost all of us are
developers here--

00:10:54.110 --> 00:10:55.250
what's in it for you?

00:10:55.250 --> 00:10:59.070
So let's look at that from
a developer's perspective.

00:10:59.070 --> 00:11:03.590
So the first thing, again,
is deployment options.

00:11:03.590 --> 00:11:07.520
If you want to run your app in
a VM, we have Compute Engine.

00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:09.050
If you switch to
containers and you

00:11:09.050 --> 00:11:10.780
want to have your
container cluster,

00:11:10.780 --> 00:11:12.732
we have Container Engine.

00:11:12.732 --> 00:11:14.440
If you don't care
about containers or VMs

00:11:14.440 --> 00:11:15.939
and you just want
to deploy your app

00:11:15.939 --> 00:11:18.440
and let someone else take
care of scaling it for you,

00:11:18.440 --> 00:11:19.976
then you have App Engine.

00:11:19.976 --> 00:11:21.350
If you don't even
care about apps

00:11:21.350 --> 00:11:23.266
and you just want to
write your functions that

00:11:23.266 --> 00:11:26.600
gets triggered by some trigger,
then you have Cloud Functions.

00:11:26.600 --> 00:11:28.940
So no matter what you
want to do at what level,

00:11:28.940 --> 00:11:31.010
we have options for you.

00:11:31.010 --> 00:11:33.050
The second thing
is, many services.

00:11:33.050 --> 00:11:35.397
So once you have your
app running in the cloud,

00:11:35.397 --> 00:11:37.730
there's all these services
that becomes available to you

00:11:37.730 --> 00:11:38.667
right away.

00:11:38.667 --> 00:11:41.000
And the key thing about these
services that we try to do

00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:44.090
is we try to make them
as managed as possible.

00:11:44.090 --> 00:11:46.380
By that I mean
you-- for example,

00:11:46.380 --> 00:11:48.410
take Pub/Sub as an example.

00:11:48.410 --> 00:11:50.990
And Pub/Sub is a public
subscribe messaging framework.

00:11:50.990 --> 00:11:53.900
So you create a
topic, and then you

00:11:53.900 --> 00:11:55.640
push messages to
that topic, and then

00:11:55.640 --> 00:11:57.440
you get messages
from that topic.

00:11:57.440 --> 00:12:00.380
Normally if you set up
a cluster or Pub/Sub,

00:12:00.380 --> 00:12:01.814
you need to worry
about machines.

00:12:01.814 --> 00:12:03.230
You need to worry
about how you're

00:12:03.230 --> 00:12:07.250
going to globally distribute
that queue and stuff like that.

00:12:07.250 --> 00:12:09.440
Whereas in Google,
Pub/Sub basically just

00:12:09.440 --> 00:12:12.050
creates the topic, and
all of the infrastructure

00:12:12.050 --> 00:12:13.670
is abstracted away from you.

00:12:13.670 --> 00:12:16.070
You don't care about how many
machines it's running on,

00:12:16.070 --> 00:12:17.910
or you don't care
where it's running.

00:12:17.910 --> 00:12:19.760
It's globally scaled
for you automatically.

00:12:19.760 --> 00:12:21.840
So this is true for BigQuery.

00:12:21.840 --> 00:12:23.120
This is true for BigTable.

00:12:23.120 --> 00:12:27.680
So we try to make it as
devop-less as possible for you.

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:29.540
And then finally
most importantly I

00:12:29.540 --> 00:12:31.550
think in my mind is
machine learning.

00:12:31.550 --> 00:12:33.660
So machine running is
really big at Google.

00:12:33.660 --> 00:12:36.470
We use it in most
all our products.

00:12:36.470 --> 00:12:40.580
And we have all these models
built up using the data

00:12:40.580 --> 00:12:41.720
that we have.

00:12:41.720 --> 00:12:43.960
And now we are
providing this machine

00:12:43.960 --> 00:12:45.930
learning to you through APIs.

00:12:45.930 --> 00:12:46.910
So we have Vision API.

00:12:46.910 --> 00:12:48.576
We have Natural
Language Processing API.

00:12:48.576 --> 00:12:49.820
We have Speech API.

00:12:49.820 --> 00:12:52.910
So we try to basically
make it really easy

00:12:52.910 --> 00:12:55.190
for you to consume machine
learning from your apps.

00:12:55.190 --> 00:12:57.150
And I'm actually
going to show you,

00:12:57.150 --> 00:13:00.530
in the demo, an example
of how to do that.

00:13:00.530 --> 00:13:03.110
And if you want to build your
own machine learning models,

00:13:03.110 --> 00:13:05.360
you can run
TensorFlow, at scale,

00:13:05.360 --> 00:13:07.120
using Google Cloud
Machine Learning.

00:13:11.510 --> 00:13:14.810
So the last thing that I want
to talk about before I show you

00:13:14.810 --> 00:13:16.830
code and demos is
what options do

00:13:16.830 --> 00:13:19.310
we have as an ASP.NET
developer when you

00:13:19.310 --> 00:13:21.260
want to deploy to Google Cloud.

00:13:21.260 --> 00:13:26.570
So if you are a
traditional ASP.NET person,

00:13:26.570 --> 00:13:30.170
your app is running in ASP.NET
on Windows, you can take that

00:13:30.170 --> 00:13:33.000
and you can host that on Compute
Engine, and it's good to go.

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:36.389
So it's running in Compute
Engine in a VM in Google Cloud.

00:13:36.389 --> 00:13:38.180
And the sample that
I'm going to show today

00:13:38.180 --> 00:13:40.940
is going to show how to do this.

00:13:40.940 --> 00:13:43.760
And then if you made
the switch from ASP.NET

00:13:43.760 --> 00:13:46.700
to ASP.NET Core running on
Linux then you have more choice.

00:13:46.700 --> 00:13:49.760
You can run it on App
Engine flexible environment,

00:13:49.760 --> 00:13:51.910
or you can run it
on Container Engine.

00:13:51.910 --> 00:13:53.540
So in App Engine
flexible environment,

00:13:53.540 --> 00:13:56.270
you basically take us
your ASP.NET Core app,

00:13:56.270 --> 00:13:59.330
put it in a Docker container,
and just say to App Engine,

00:13:59.330 --> 00:14:02.180
just take this container
and run it for me at scale.

00:14:02.180 --> 00:14:05.080
So App Engine kind of figures
out what kind of scale

00:14:05.080 --> 00:14:06.950
do you need, and it
provisions machines.

00:14:06.950 --> 00:14:08.330
And it load balances.

00:14:08.330 --> 00:14:10.577
And it has versioning.

00:14:10.577 --> 00:14:11.660
It does traffic splitting.

00:14:11.660 --> 00:14:14.000
So there's all this
goodness built in.

00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:16.650
All you need to do is just wrap
your application into a Docker

00:14:16.650 --> 00:14:17.810
container and just
give it to it,

00:14:17.810 --> 00:14:19.310
and it will just
run at it scale.

00:14:19.310 --> 00:14:20.600
It's very easy.

00:14:20.600 --> 00:14:22.190
And the session
in this afternoon

00:14:22.190 --> 00:14:26.060
will show this App Engine
flexible environment later.

00:14:26.060 --> 00:14:29.450
If you want to build your
own container cluster,

00:14:29.450 --> 00:14:32.330
you can also wrap your
ASP.NET core application

00:14:32.330 --> 00:14:35.642
into a container and run it on
Kubernetes in Container Engine.

00:14:35.642 --> 00:14:37.100
So this gives you
more flexibility.

00:14:37.100 --> 00:14:39.260
It's more involved
than App Engine.

00:14:39.260 --> 00:14:41.310
But with that you
have more flexibility

00:14:41.310 --> 00:14:46.390
what you can run in
your container cluster.

00:14:46.390 --> 00:14:47.670
So this is for the upside.

00:14:47.670 --> 00:14:51.376
So for data, if you're
using SQL Server,

00:14:51.376 --> 00:14:53.750
you can take the SQL Server
and run it on Compute Engine.

00:14:53.750 --> 00:14:59.372
And we support all the
versions from 2008 until 2016.

00:14:59.372 --> 00:15:00.330
And then we have tools.

00:15:00.330 --> 00:15:02.160
So we have a Visual
Studio plugin

00:15:02.160 --> 00:15:04.620
where you can manage
your Google Cloud

00:15:04.620 --> 00:15:06.810
resources from Visual Studio.

00:15:06.810 --> 00:15:11.040
We have lots of .NET libraries
to talk to pretty much all

00:15:11.040 --> 00:15:12.930
Google Cloud Services.

00:15:12.930 --> 00:15:15.000
And then finally we
have PowerShell cmdlets

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:18.390
that lets you script your
access using PowerShell.

00:15:18.390 --> 00:15:19.960
So no matter what
you want to do--

00:15:19.960 --> 00:15:22.680
ASP.NET, ASP.NET
Core, SQL Server,

00:15:22.680 --> 00:15:26.700
tools-- we try to basically
make that available to you.

00:15:26.700 --> 00:15:29.220
And there's more and more
stuff that are coming.

00:15:32.230 --> 00:15:33.680
So that was enough talk.

00:15:33.680 --> 00:15:37.242
Now it's time to show you
some code and some demos.

00:15:37.242 --> 00:15:39.200
So in this part of the
talk, I want to show you

00:15:39.200 --> 00:15:40.560
a simple application.

00:15:40.560 --> 00:15:42.650
First we're going to
run it on my laptop.

00:15:42.650 --> 00:15:45.925
And then once it's
working on my laptop,

00:15:45.925 --> 00:15:48.050
I want to show you how we
can take that application

00:15:48.050 --> 00:15:50.690
and move it to Google Cloud.

00:15:50.690 --> 00:15:52.960
The application that
I want to show you,

00:15:52.960 --> 00:15:54.490
it's called Contoso University.

00:15:54.490 --> 00:15:56.330
Maybe some of you
know this application?

00:15:56.330 --> 00:15:57.710
Anyone knows?

00:15:57.710 --> 00:15:58.230
No?

00:15:58.230 --> 00:15:58.730
Maybe?

00:15:58.730 --> 00:15:59.480
OK.

00:15:59.480 --> 00:16:03.380
So Contoso University, it's
a fictitious university.

00:16:03.380 --> 00:16:09.290
It's a reference application
for ASP.NET MVC 5.

00:16:09.290 --> 00:16:14.990
So it does some simple pages
for students, for courses,

00:16:14.990 --> 00:16:16.580
for instructors.

00:16:16.580 --> 00:16:19.610
And it's backed by a
SQL Server instance.

00:16:19.610 --> 00:16:24.410
And it's using MVC, so
model-view-controller 5.

00:16:24.410 --> 00:16:26.360
So it's just a
reference application

00:16:26.360 --> 00:16:29.600
showing how to use MVC
backed by a SQL Server.

00:16:29.600 --> 00:16:31.650
So let's just take a
look at that quickly.

00:16:31.650 --> 00:16:35.387
So in the demo I just want to
show you how this works locally

00:16:35.387 --> 00:16:35.970
on my machine.

00:16:38.490 --> 00:16:40.620
So I already have the
Contoso University solution

00:16:40.620 --> 00:16:42.450
open on my machine.

00:16:42.450 --> 00:16:46.844
And as I mentioned, this
is an MVC application.

00:16:46.844 --> 00:16:48.760
Well I already have it
running, so let me just

00:16:48.760 --> 00:16:50.920
show you that quickly here.

00:16:50.920 --> 00:16:53.320
So this is running
locally on my machine.

00:16:53.320 --> 00:16:57.800
If you go to students, this
is showing the students.

00:16:57.800 --> 00:16:58.860
It's paged.

00:16:58.860 --> 00:17:02.520
You can look at the
details of the student.

00:17:02.520 --> 00:17:05.316
We have courses.

00:17:05.316 --> 00:17:08.700
And then we have instructors.

00:17:08.700 --> 00:17:11.819
So it's pretty basic.

00:17:11.819 --> 00:17:15.569
And then just to show
you code a little bit,

00:17:15.569 --> 00:17:19.040
so we have-- let's
start with models.

00:17:19.040 --> 00:17:25.250
So we have models for
student, course, department.

00:17:25.250 --> 00:17:28.440
If we look at the
person for example,

00:17:28.440 --> 00:17:31.460
this has last name,
first name, full name.

00:17:31.460 --> 00:17:34.240
And then student
extends from the person.

00:17:34.240 --> 00:17:37.060
And on top of that it adds
enrollment date, enrollment.

00:17:37.060 --> 00:17:40.010
So you know which student
belongs to which class,

00:17:40.010 --> 00:17:41.500
stuff like that.

00:17:41.500 --> 00:17:43.130
As you know in MVC
applications, you

00:17:43.130 --> 00:17:45.440
have a controller that
actually controls the model.

00:17:45.440 --> 00:17:49.940
So let's look at the
student controller.

00:17:49.940 --> 00:17:57.950
So in StudentController, we have
basic methods for HTTP calls.

00:17:57.950 --> 00:18:01.100
So for example, if you
get a detail or a student,

00:18:01.100 --> 00:18:02.930
then this method gets
called with an ID.

00:18:06.090 --> 00:18:08.157
And then we have a
db, database object.

00:18:08.157 --> 00:18:09.740
And then from there
we find a student.

00:18:09.740 --> 00:18:11.450
And then we just
return the view.

00:18:11.450 --> 00:18:14.435
And then we have a bunch of
other methods for create,

00:18:14.435 --> 00:18:16.260
creating a student,
editing a student,

00:18:16.260 --> 00:18:19.380
and so on and so forth.

00:18:19.380 --> 00:18:20.884
So we covered model.

00:18:20.884 --> 00:18:21.800
We covered controller.

00:18:21.800 --> 00:18:22.910
And then we have views.

00:18:22.910 --> 00:18:26.584
So if you look at
the views folder,

00:18:26.584 --> 00:18:28.000
we have separate
views for a bunch

00:18:28.000 --> 00:18:29.950
of different things like
courses, departments, home.

00:18:29.950 --> 00:18:31.616
But then if you look
at the student one,

00:18:31.616 --> 00:18:33.100
it's basically just HTML.

00:18:33.100 --> 00:18:35.980
Let's look at the
details page for example.

00:18:35.980 --> 00:18:41.200
It's basically HTML with
some ASP.NET stuff scattered

00:18:41.200 --> 00:18:43.240
in there.

00:18:43.240 --> 00:18:45.970
And then one last thing
to show you is Web.config.

00:18:50.880 --> 00:18:51.540
Oh sorry, here.

00:18:54.100 --> 00:19:00.080
So in Web.config,
there is a LocalDb.

00:19:02.750 --> 00:19:05.570
So the actual database is local.

00:19:05.570 --> 00:19:07.999
So it's the SQL Server
running locally on my machine.

00:19:07.999 --> 00:19:10.040
But we're going to change
that later to use a SQL

00:19:10.040 --> 00:19:11.430
Server running on Google Cloud.

00:19:14.620 --> 00:19:15.970
So this is the app.

00:19:15.970 --> 00:19:18.520
Let me just go back to
my presentation quickly.

00:19:21.170 --> 00:19:23.787
All right, so now I want to
take this app, running locally,

00:19:23.787 --> 00:19:25.370
and I want to push
it to Google Cloud.

00:19:25.370 --> 00:19:28.190
So let's see what steps
I need to do that.

00:19:28.190 --> 00:19:30.860
First, I'm going to make
life easy for myself

00:19:30.860 --> 00:19:33.350
and I'm going to install
Google Cloud for Visual Studio.

00:19:33.350 --> 00:19:38.090
This will enable me to interface
with Google Cloud, create

00:19:38.090 --> 00:19:40.770
VMs, and stuff like that.

00:19:40.770 --> 00:19:44.840
Once I have that, this is
a traditional ASP.NET app.

00:19:44.840 --> 00:19:47.540
So I need a Windows VM
with ASP.NET framework

00:19:47.540 --> 00:19:49.040
already installed on it.

00:19:49.040 --> 00:19:51.440
So I'm going to use
Visual Studio plugin

00:19:51.440 --> 00:19:54.650
to get a VM that I need.

00:19:54.650 --> 00:19:56.240
We also need a SQL
Server instance,

00:19:56.240 --> 00:19:58.640
because the one that I'm
using right now is local.

00:19:58.640 --> 00:20:01.760
So I'm going to get a SQL Server
instance on Compute Engine.

00:20:01.760 --> 00:20:04.079
And then finally, I'm
going to publish directly

00:20:04.079 --> 00:20:04.870
from Visual Studio.

00:20:04.870 --> 00:20:07.290
So from Visual Studio I'm just
going to publish to the VM

00:20:07.290 --> 00:20:08.220
that I just created.

00:20:08.220 --> 00:20:09.740
So these are the steps.

00:20:09.740 --> 00:20:12.170
So let's just take a look.

00:20:12.170 --> 00:20:13.930
So in this part I'm going to--

00:20:17.430 --> 00:20:18.780
first let me stop this.

00:20:21.920 --> 00:20:27.590
So if you go to Tools, and
then Extensions and Updates,

00:20:27.590 --> 00:20:30.690
this is where you can
install Visual Studio plug-in

00:20:30.690 --> 00:20:31.940
that we have for Google Cloud.

00:20:31.940 --> 00:20:33.380
So I'll go here.

00:20:33.380 --> 00:20:35.066
I click on Online.

00:20:35.066 --> 00:20:37.420
This searches Visual
Studio gallery.

00:20:37.420 --> 00:20:41.405
So here I'm just going
to type, Google Cloud.

00:20:44.280 --> 00:20:46.500
And as you can see, this
is the extension I want.

00:20:46.500 --> 00:20:48.900
It's already installed for
me, because I have it here.

00:20:48.900 --> 00:20:50.480
But if it's not,
you can double click

00:20:50.480 --> 00:20:51.896
and it will install
it right here.

00:20:51.896 --> 00:20:53.910
Then you need to
restart Visual Studio.

00:20:53.910 --> 00:20:56.624
So once you do that,
you go to Tools.

00:20:56.624 --> 00:20:58.290
And then you see this
Google Cloud tool.

00:20:58.290 --> 00:20:59.880
So this is the
plug-in that basically

00:20:59.880 --> 00:21:02.670
lets you access Google Cloud.

00:21:02.670 --> 00:21:04.140
First you need to
add your account

00:21:04.140 --> 00:21:05.820
so that Visual Studio
is authenticated

00:21:05.820 --> 00:21:07.950
with Google Cloud.

00:21:07.950 --> 00:21:12.660
You go to Accounts,
and then Add Account.

00:21:12.660 --> 00:21:15.440
This opens a new
browser and it asks me,

00:21:15.440 --> 00:21:17.010
which account do
you want to use?

00:21:17.010 --> 00:21:18.551
I want to use my
development account.

00:21:21.220 --> 00:21:23.290
And then Allow.

00:21:23.290 --> 00:21:25.740
And now Visual
Studio is authorized

00:21:25.740 --> 00:21:29.170
to access my account,
which is great.

00:21:29.170 --> 00:21:30.250
I go back here.

00:21:30.250 --> 00:21:32.370
It tells me that your account is
already here, because I already

00:21:32.370 --> 00:21:33.286
added my account here.

00:21:33.286 --> 00:21:34.800
That's OK.

00:21:34.800 --> 00:21:39.760
And now if I go to Tools
and Google Cloud Tools,

00:21:39.760 --> 00:21:42.400
I do Show Google Cloud Explorer.

00:21:42.400 --> 00:21:43.810
Now this is me.

00:21:43.810 --> 00:21:47.000
And then these are my projects
that I have under my account.

00:21:47.000 --> 00:21:50.170
I'm going to look at the
.NET project that I have.

00:21:50.170 --> 00:21:53.920
Under here I can see all the
VM instances that I have,

00:21:53.920 --> 00:21:55.690
all the storage and SQL.

00:21:55.690 --> 00:21:58.620
So there is going to
be more stuff here.

00:21:58.620 --> 00:22:00.350
And it's coming up.

00:22:00.350 --> 00:22:03.209
But for now we just show Compute
Engine, clusters, Cloud SQL.

00:22:03.209 --> 00:22:04.750
But in the feature
you basically will

00:22:04.750 --> 00:22:07.600
be able to see all your
resources from here.

00:22:07.600 --> 00:22:10.630
So what I need to do here
is first, as I told you,

00:22:10.630 --> 00:22:14.080
I need to have a Windows
VM with ASP.NET framework.

00:22:14.080 --> 00:22:15.495
So let's try to get that.

00:22:15.495 --> 00:22:16.870
What I'm going to
do is I'm going

00:22:16.870 --> 00:22:19.450
to right click here and just
say new ASP.NET instance.

00:22:23.780 --> 00:22:26.350
And this takes me to
Google Cloud Console.

00:22:26.350 --> 00:22:29.020
And then there's
called Cloud Launcher,

00:22:29.020 --> 00:22:32.170
which is like a marketplace
for solutions on Google Cloud.

00:22:32.170 --> 00:22:34.660
And one of those solutions
is called ASP.NET Framework.

00:22:34.660 --> 00:22:38.890
So this will enable me
to install Windows--

00:22:38.890 --> 00:22:42.690
get a Windows VM with ASP.NET
framework with basically one

00:22:42.690 --> 00:22:43.190
click.

00:22:43.190 --> 00:22:45.273
So I'm just going to say
Launch on Compute Engine.

00:22:47.390 --> 00:22:53.810
And then let's just call this
ASP.NET GCP Next San Francisco.

00:22:53.810 --> 00:22:55.430
You can choose your zone.

00:22:55.430 --> 00:22:57.440
I just choose US West.

00:22:57.440 --> 00:22:59.510
You can customize
your machine type.

00:22:59.510 --> 00:23:02.090
You have like 2008, 2012, 2016.

00:23:02.090 --> 00:23:04.080
I'll choose 2016.

00:23:04.080 --> 00:23:06.080
And then you can
customize your disk size,

00:23:06.080 --> 00:23:07.260
all that kind of stuff.

00:23:07.260 --> 00:23:08.810
And then I just hit Deploy.

00:23:08.810 --> 00:23:10.502
And now within a
couple of minutes,

00:23:10.502 --> 00:23:12.710
this will give me a Windows
VM with ASP.NET framework

00:23:12.710 --> 00:23:13.760
installed.

00:23:13.760 --> 00:23:17.330
Now I already have one, so let
me just show you that quickly.

00:23:17.330 --> 00:23:20.630
If I go under Compute
Engine, I already

00:23:20.630 --> 00:23:25.100
have this
contoso-original as my VM.

