00:38:50.570 --> 00:38:52.730
So as you can see, there's
some fun facts here

00:38:52.730 --> 00:38:55.700
that you can add some
text about the student.

00:38:55.700 --> 00:38:58.550
What I want to do is
have this Record button.

00:38:58.550 --> 00:39:00.170
You can click on
it and talk to it,

00:39:00.170 --> 00:39:03.200
and it will detect your
voice, convert it to text,

00:39:03.200 --> 00:39:05.010
and then edit here.

00:39:05.010 --> 00:39:11.090
So to do that, what
I did is I created

00:39:11.090 --> 00:39:12.480
a couple of more services.

00:39:12.480 --> 00:39:15.590
So the first one is
called VoiceRecorder.

00:39:15.590 --> 00:39:17.780
So this is not that interesting.

00:39:17.780 --> 00:39:23.320
It basically has a method
called RecordFromMicrophone,

00:39:23.320 --> 00:39:24.040
with seconds.

00:39:24.040 --> 00:39:27.070
So you provide some seconds,
like say three seconds,

00:39:27.070 --> 00:39:30.100
and it records your voice from
microphone for three seconds.

00:39:30.100 --> 00:39:31.420
And it saves it to a file.

00:39:31.420 --> 00:39:32.230
That's all it does.

00:39:32.230 --> 00:39:34.210
So there's nothing fancy here.

00:39:34.210 --> 00:39:36.940
But the other one,
VoiceRecognizer, it's

00:39:36.940 --> 00:39:38.140
much more interesting.

00:39:38.140 --> 00:39:43.780
First, it's using the
Google Cloud Speech library.

00:39:43.780 --> 00:39:47.080
And then it has something
called SpeechClient.

00:39:47.080 --> 00:39:49.315
So as you can see, all
the services that we have,

00:39:49.315 --> 00:39:50.440
they have the same pattern.

00:39:50.440 --> 00:39:51.607
They have a NuGet package.

00:39:51.607 --> 00:39:53.440
And then they have a
client that you create.

00:39:53.440 --> 00:39:55.340
And then you use that
client to talk to it.

00:39:55.340 --> 00:39:57.610
So in this case, it's
the Speech client.

00:39:57.610 --> 00:39:59.980
And then it has a
method called Recognize.

00:39:59.980 --> 00:40:02.140
So what it does is
that it basically just,

00:40:02.140 --> 00:40:06.610
using the Speech client, it
calls SyncRecognizeAsync,

00:40:06.610 --> 00:40:08.590
and pass in some
parameters, that

00:40:08.590 --> 00:40:10.340
are usually the default ones.

00:40:10.340 --> 00:40:13.030
And then you provide a
file path, so the file path

00:40:13.030 --> 00:40:15.310
of your recording.

00:40:15.310 --> 00:40:20.050
And then, this gets sent to
the Speech API in Google Cloud.

00:40:20.050 --> 00:40:21.940
And that returns some results.

00:40:21.940 --> 00:40:26.560
So usually when you pass in your
voice, you have one result back

00:40:26.560 --> 00:40:27.790
and you could just use that.

00:40:27.790 --> 00:40:31.930
But sometimes depending on
how clear your voice is,

00:40:31.930 --> 00:40:36.130
you might have multiple results
with multiple alternatives.

00:40:36.130 --> 00:40:39.719
And each alternative has
its own confidence level.

00:40:39.719 --> 00:40:41.260
So you would look
at the alternatives

00:40:41.260 --> 00:40:43.660
and pick the one that has
the highest confidence.

00:40:43.660 --> 00:40:45.190
But in this case,
I'm just looking

00:40:45.190 --> 00:40:47.050
at the first alternative
and just returning

00:40:47.050 --> 00:40:50.565
that, the first one,
because this is just a demo.

00:40:50.565 --> 00:40:52.530
All right, that's good.

00:40:52.530 --> 00:40:58.280
And then what I lastly changed
is in StudentController,

00:40:58.280 --> 00:41:03.650
I created a new
method called Record.

00:41:03.650 --> 00:41:05.140
So when you click
that button, it

00:41:05.140 --> 00:41:08.879
gets called here,
with the student ID.

00:41:08.879 --> 00:41:09.670
I find the student.

00:41:13.430 --> 00:41:16.280
And then I use the voice
recorder to record my voice

00:41:16.280 --> 00:41:17.590
for three seconds.

00:41:17.590 --> 00:41:19.260
That saves to a file.

00:41:19.260 --> 00:41:21.410
Then I pass that file
to a VoiceRecognizer

00:41:21.410 --> 00:41:24.770
that I just wrote, and
just say Recognize.

00:41:24.770 --> 00:41:27.740
And that gives me, from
the voice, the text.

00:41:27.740 --> 00:41:29.600
I get the text back.

00:41:29.600 --> 00:41:32.720
And then I just set that as
the fun facts of the students.

00:41:32.720 --> 00:41:35.920
So let's try this out.

00:41:35.920 --> 00:41:37.840
So here I'm just going
to say something.

00:41:37.840 --> 00:41:40.240
Let's see if it works.

00:41:40.240 --> 00:41:41.170
He is cool.

00:41:47.820 --> 00:41:50.420
Oh, I don't know if it worked.

00:41:50.420 --> 00:41:51.380
I'll try one more time.

00:41:51.380 --> 00:41:55.780
Maybe the microphone
is not hooked in.

00:41:55.780 --> 00:41:56.525
He is cool.

00:42:01.470 --> 00:42:04.020
Ah, yeah, it worked, cool.

00:42:04.020 --> 00:42:05.780
So hit Save.

00:42:05.780 --> 00:42:06.840
And that's it.

00:42:06.840 --> 00:42:14.200
All right, so I'll go
back to my presentation.

00:42:14.200 --> 00:42:17.200
And let's see, I
have some time left.

00:42:17.200 --> 00:42:18.370
So this is fun.

00:42:18.370 --> 00:42:21.910
As you can see, we moved our
application to the cloud.

00:42:21.910 --> 00:42:25.130
And with that, we get all
these services almost for free.

00:42:25.130 --> 00:42:26.980
And then with a couple
of lines of code,

00:42:26.980 --> 00:42:30.040
we use machine learning
to detect images

00:42:30.040 --> 00:42:33.160
if they are bad images.

00:42:33.160 --> 00:42:36.654
And then we did speech to text.

00:42:36.654 --> 00:42:39.070
But now you need to take your
application into production,

00:42:39.070 --> 00:42:39.790
right?

00:42:39.790 --> 00:42:42.190
We have some problems
with this application.

00:42:42.190 --> 00:42:45.220
For example, first of
all, this application

00:42:45.220 --> 00:42:48.080
just runs on one
VM in the cloud.

00:42:48.080 --> 00:42:51.270
If that VM goes down, you
have no way of knowing that.

00:42:51.270 --> 00:42:53.190
So what you actually
need is health checks.

00:42:53.190 --> 00:42:54.940
You need to add health
checks to make sure

00:42:54.940 --> 00:42:59.170
that you know if your
application is healthy or not.

00:42:59.170 --> 00:43:00.580
There's no logging right now.

00:43:00.580 --> 00:43:02.479
So you're kind of blind.

00:43:02.479 --> 00:43:04.770
Thankfully there's something
called Stackdriver Logging

00:43:04.770 --> 00:43:08.320
in Google Cloud that gives
you centralized logging,

00:43:08.320 --> 00:43:11.110
not just in Google Cloud, but
across other cloud providers

00:43:11.110 --> 00:43:11.750
as well.

00:43:11.750 --> 00:43:15.746
So we'll start Stackdriver
Logging to our application.

00:43:15.746 --> 00:43:16.870
There's no error reporting.

00:43:16.870 --> 00:43:20.044
So if there's an error, if
we don't know about that,

00:43:20.044 --> 00:43:22.210
there's something called
Stackdriver Error Reporting

00:43:22.210 --> 00:43:24.730
that basically captures all
the errors in your application

00:43:24.730 --> 00:43:27.490
and then gives you a
centralized dashboard

00:43:27.490 --> 00:43:30.947
to look at all your errors, when
they happened, how often they

00:43:30.947 --> 00:43:32.030
happened, stuff like that.

00:43:32.030 --> 00:43:34.000
So we'll add that.

00:43:34.000 --> 00:43:36.550
And then our application
is not resilient

00:43:36.550 --> 00:43:38.270
because it's just one instance.

00:43:38.270 --> 00:43:43.780
If we have more load
coming in, one instance

00:43:43.780 --> 00:43:44.920
is not going to cover that.

00:43:44.920 --> 00:43:47.680
We need multiple
instances, load balanced.

00:43:47.680 --> 00:43:50.997
And we need autoscaling
actually to make this work.

00:43:50.997 --> 00:43:53.080
Thankfully there's something
called instant groups

00:43:53.080 --> 00:43:55.510
in Google Cloud that
makes this really easy.

00:43:55.510 --> 00:43:57.270
So we're going to
add an instance group

00:43:57.270 --> 00:44:00.220
and have all of this
happen magically for us.

00:44:00.220 --> 00:44:05.500
So just to talk about the
scaling in Google Cloud,

00:44:05.500 --> 00:44:07.210
so this is what
we had initially.

00:44:07.210 --> 00:44:09.710
I had my application
running locally.

00:44:09.710 --> 00:44:13.930
Then I pushed it to a single
VM running in Google Cloud.

00:44:13.930 --> 00:44:15.790
So what you can do
from that point on

00:44:15.790 --> 00:44:19.840
is that you can take that VM
and create a snapshot out of it.

00:44:19.840 --> 00:44:21.940
It's called an instance image.

00:44:21.940 --> 00:44:24.780
So this is kind of like a
snapshot of your application.

00:44:24.780 --> 00:44:27.410
And then from the image, you
create an instance template.

00:44:27.410 --> 00:44:32.740
So a template is like a
blueprint for your application.

00:44:32.740 --> 00:44:34.510
It's a blueprint of
how you can create

00:44:34.510 --> 00:44:36.580
the same app on a different VM.

00:44:36.580 --> 00:44:38.770
So once you have the
instance template,

00:44:38.770 --> 00:44:40.780
from there you can
create an instance group.

00:44:40.780 --> 00:44:42.490
And this is where all
the magic happens.

00:44:42.490 --> 00:44:44.950
Basically with
instance group, you

00:44:44.950 --> 00:44:48.140
will get multiple VMs, load
balanced behind that load

00:44:48.140 --> 00:44:49.004
balancer.

00:44:49.004 --> 00:44:50.170
And they have health checks.

00:44:50.170 --> 00:44:53.590
So the load balancer uses the
health check to check your app.

00:44:53.590 --> 00:44:56.710
And then it also
has autoscaling.

00:44:56.710 --> 00:44:59.140
So if you need to have
more than one instance,

00:44:59.140 --> 00:45:00.730
it will automatically scale.

00:45:00.730 --> 00:45:03.010
So an instance group basically
gives you all the stuff

00:45:03.010 --> 00:45:06.940
that you need to run your
application in production.

00:45:06.940 --> 00:45:08.430
I'm just going to
show you quickly

00:45:08.430 --> 00:45:12.290
what are the steps for
you to set this up.

00:45:12.290 --> 00:45:20.690
All right, so let me first
show you the health check.

00:45:20.690 --> 00:45:23.360
First, for the health check,
I created a new controller

00:45:23.360 --> 00:45:25.220
called PingController.

00:45:25.220 --> 00:45:28.200
So this PingController,
it's very simple right now.

00:45:28.200 --> 00:45:32.390
Basically when you hit the
endpoint, you get a pong back.

00:45:32.390 --> 00:45:34.670
Normally in a real
world application,

00:45:34.670 --> 00:45:36.650
you want to check
your dependencies

00:45:36.650 --> 00:45:40.010
and make sure they're healthy
before you can return OK.

00:45:40.010 --> 00:45:44.660
But in this case, I'm just
returning OK no matter what.

00:45:44.660 --> 00:45:48.230
I also created another
endpoint with some ID.

00:45:48.230 --> 00:45:49.160
And I am just saying--

00:45:49.160 --> 00:45:50.660
I'm just throwing
an error because I

00:45:50.660 --> 00:45:53.840
want to show you how this works
in Google Stackdriver Error

00:45:53.840 --> 00:45:54.841
Reporting.

00:45:54.841 --> 00:45:56.090
So I have this PingController.

00:45:56.090 --> 00:46:02.390
And actually if I go back to my
app, I think it was this one.

00:46:02.390 --> 00:46:05.340
And if I do api/ping,
yeah, it just

00:46:05.340 --> 00:46:07.470
tells me pong,
nothing interesting.

00:46:07.470 --> 00:46:10.590
But what you can do is you can
go to Google Cloud Console,

00:46:10.590 --> 00:46:16.700
and under Compute Engine,
and then Health Checks,

00:46:16.700 --> 00:46:18.350
you can create a health check.

00:46:18.350 --> 00:46:19.160
I already have one.

00:46:19.160 --> 00:46:22.430
But let me just show you how
that health check looks like.

00:46:22.430 --> 00:46:26.090
You basically just say,
this is an HTTP health check

00:46:26.090 --> 00:46:27.440
with port 80.

00:46:27.440 --> 00:46:29.030
And this is the
endpoint that I want

00:46:29.030 --> 00:46:31.430
to hit to make sure
that it's healthy.

00:46:31.430 --> 00:46:35.330
And then these are the
criteria for my health check.

00:46:35.330 --> 00:46:38.810
So I'm going to check
every 30 seconds.

00:46:38.810 --> 00:46:40.834
And if I get OK two
times, I'm going

00:46:40.834 --> 00:46:42.000
to assume that it's healthy.

00:46:42.000 --> 00:46:45.020
If I get errors two
times, I'm going

00:46:45.020 --> 00:46:46.950
to assume it's not
healthy, stuff like that.

00:46:46.950 --> 00:46:48.366
So what we're going
to do is we're

00:46:48.366 --> 00:46:51.337
going to use this health check
to create our instance group.

00:46:51.337 --> 00:46:53.920
And then when the load balancer
gets created with the instance

00:46:53.920 --> 00:46:55.419
group, it will use
this health check

00:46:55.419 --> 00:46:58.010
to determine whether
our instance is healthy.

00:46:58.010 --> 00:47:00.670
So that's one.

00:47:00.670 --> 00:47:02.060
Let me go here.

00:47:02.060 --> 00:47:04.620
Second thing I want to
show you is Stackdriver.

00:47:04.620 --> 00:47:06.070
So let me just find the code.

00:47:12.130 --> 00:47:17.320
So first, I created something
called StackdriverLogger.

00:47:17.320 --> 00:47:20.470
So the original logger
of the application,

00:47:20.470 --> 00:47:22.410
it basically just uses trace.

00:47:22.410 --> 00:47:23.560
But I don't want trace.

00:47:23.560 --> 00:47:25.990
I want my logs to go
to a central place

00:47:25.990 --> 00:47:28.180
so I can look at them.

00:47:28.180 --> 00:47:29.860
So with Stackdriver,
all I had to do

00:47:29.860 --> 00:47:32.170
was, again, use
the NuGet package.

00:47:32.170 --> 00:47:34.990
So this is the NuGet package
for Stackdriver logging.

00:47:34.990 --> 00:47:37.830
And then from there, I
just create a client.

00:47:37.830 --> 00:47:40.600
Again, it's the same
kind of paradigm

00:47:40.600 --> 00:47:42.460
you referenced in
the NuGet package.

00:47:42.460 --> 00:47:43.790
You have a client.

00:47:43.790 --> 00:47:46.630
So once I have the client,
I just use this to log.

00:47:46.630 --> 00:47:50.200
So by logging with this
client, I have my logs central

00:47:50.200 --> 00:47:51.430
in Google Cloud.

00:47:51.430 --> 00:47:52.610
So that's number one.

00:47:52.610 --> 00:47:54.110
The second thing
is error reporting.

00:47:54.110 --> 00:47:56.740
So I want all my errors
to be caught and sent

00:47:56.740 --> 00:47:57.820
to Google Cloud.

00:47:57.820 --> 00:48:02.084
So to do that, in
WebApiConfig, I just registered

00:48:02.084 --> 00:48:03.250
Stackdriver Error Reporting.

00:48:03.250 --> 00:48:06.150
So it's basically
just one line of code.

00:48:06.150 --> 00:48:08.440
And all I'm saying
here is that when

00:48:08.440 --> 00:48:10.840
there's an exception,
this kind of exception,

00:48:10.840 --> 00:48:15.220
which is like the
main exception class,

00:48:15.220 --> 00:48:18.890
just basically just report
that exception to this project,

00:48:18.890 --> 00:48:20.810
to this service,
to this version.

00:48:20.810 --> 00:48:23.220
So everything that's
thrown from my application

00:48:23.220 --> 00:48:25.900
will be caught and
sent to Google Cloud.

00:48:25.900 --> 00:48:29.210
So let's just show these
quickly in the console.

00:48:29.210 --> 00:48:34.330
So if you go back to Console,
go to Stackdriver, here.

00:48:34.330 --> 00:48:35.718
Let's look at the logging first.

00:48:39.390 --> 00:48:42.060
So Stackdriver logs
have a lot of stuff.

00:48:42.060 --> 00:48:46.020
But in this case, my
application is a GCE,

00:48:46.020 --> 00:48:48.890
Google Compute Engine instance.

00:48:48.890 --> 00:48:50.730
And this is my
application, so this

00:48:50.730 --> 00:48:52.600
is where it's going to log.

00:48:52.600 --> 00:48:58.070
And as you can see
here, these are my logs.

