it's just seen this exception.

00:49:40.510 --> 00:49:43.300
It's seen in Contoso
application version 1.

00:49:43.300 --> 00:49:46.430
And if I click on it,
I get more details.

00:49:46.430 --> 00:49:50.110
So I only have it
not work today.

00:49:50.110 --> 00:49:53.174
But if you have it
failing like a month,

00:49:53.174 --> 00:49:55.090
you can see when it
happened, how it happened,

00:49:55.090 --> 00:49:57.700
what was its stack trace,
all that kind of stuff that

00:49:57.700 --> 00:49:58.750
becomes available to you.

00:49:58.750 --> 00:50:01.000
With just one line of code,
you can catch all of this,

00:50:01.000 --> 00:50:02.070
and then it's right here.

00:50:02.070 --> 00:50:05.210
I think this is really good.

00:50:05.210 --> 00:50:07.970
And then the last thing I want
to show you is the instance

00:50:07.970 --> 00:50:10.240
groups and autoscaling.

00:50:10.240 --> 00:50:16.060
So to do that, what I had to
do is go to Compute Engine.

00:50:16.060 --> 00:50:19.180
First, I need to create a
snapshot over my application.

00:50:19.180 --> 00:50:21.020
So I need to create an image.

00:50:21.020 --> 00:50:23.500
So here, I have images.

00:50:23.500 --> 00:50:25.720
And then as you can see
there's the contoso-gcp one.

00:50:25.720 --> 00:50:27.285
This is the one that I created.

00:50:27.285 --> 00:50:29.410
There are instructions on
how to create this image,

00:50:29.410 --> 00:50:30.970
but it's pretty basic basically.

00:50:30.970 --> 00:50:34.140
You just go to your Windows
VM instance, run a command,

00:50:34.140 --> 00:50:35.300
and it shuts down.

00:50:35.300 --> 00:50:37.000
And then from
there you just say,

00:50:37.000 --> 00:50:38.830
create an image from
this persistent disk.

00:50:38.830 --> 00:50:39.830
And you have your image.

00:50:39.830 --> 00:50:42.140
And you can share that
image with other people,

00:50:42.140 --> 00:50:43.730
with other projects,
stuff like that.

00:50:43.730 --> 00:50:45.730
So once you have
this image, then you

00:50:45.730 --> 00:50:47.950
need to create a
template, a blueprint

00:50:47.950 --> 00:50:51.700
of how to create that image
and use it in other VMs.

00:50:51.700 --> 00:50:54.050
So I have instance templates.

00:50:54.050 --> 00:50:55.720
So here it is,
contoso-gcp-template

00:50:55.720 --> 00:50:57.760
that I created before this talk.

00:50:57.760 --> 00:51:00.280
And this guy, basically
the important thing

00:51:00.280 --> 00:51:03.590
is that it's using the image
I just showed you about.

00:51:03.590 --> 00:51:08.410
And it has some configuration
that you can set.

00:51:08.410 --> 00:51:09.860
And that's it.

00:51:09.860 --> 00:51:11.060
You There's not much to it.

00:51:11.060 --> 00:51:15.370
And then you can also specify
what kind of APIs and things

00:51:15.370 --> 00:51:16.510
that it can use.

00:51:19.427 --> 00:51:20.010
And that's it.

00:51:20.010 --> 00:51:21.324
So I have my template.

00:51:21.324 --> 00:51:23.740
So from this template, now I
can create an instance group.

00:51:23.740 --> 00:51:24.970
So I have five minutes left.

00:51:24.970 --> 00:51:26.660
So I just want to
show you that quickly.

00:51:26.660 --> 00:51:30.510
In Instance Groups, I
created an instance group.

00:51:30.510 --> 00:51:33.040
And if we just edit
that instance group,

00:51:33.040 --> 00:51:34.710
so this is where all
the magic happens.

00:51:34.710 --> 00:51:38.490
First, it's using the
template I created.

00:51:38.490 --> 00:51:40.110
And then autoscaling is on.

00:51:40.110 --> 00:51:41.880
So by just setting
this on, I got

00:51:41.880 --> 00:51:45.000
autoscaling, which is great.

00:51:45.000 --> 00:51:46.410
It is using load balancing.

00:51:46.410 --> 00:51:50.280
So it's HTTP load
balancing on port 80.

00:51:50.280 --> 00:51:52.950
I can specify how many
instances I want by default.

00:51:52.950 --> 00:51:55.410
But minimum, I will have
one instance by minimum.

00:51:55.410 --> 00:51:58.034
And then for this demo,
I said three instances.

00:51:58.034 --> 00:51:59.700
So it will autoscale
to three instances.

00:51:59.700 --> 00:52:02.100
But you can set that
to whatever you want.

00:52:02.100 --> 00:52:06.984
And then, it's also
using health check.

00:52:06.984 --> 00:52:09.400
So it's using the health check
that I defined to make sure

00:52:09.400 --> 00:52:11.170
that my instances are healthy.

00:52:11.170 --> 00:52:13.510
So with this, I get pretty
much everything I want.

00:52:13.510 --> 00:52:16.120
My application is much
more ready to production.

00:52:16.120 --> 00:52:19.210
And we have all the pieces
basically to make this happen.

00:52:19.210 --> 00:52:21.790
